<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">German South West Africa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a> was a colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in present-day Namibia. German forces there carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the Herero and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nama-people">Nama peoples</a> in 1904–1908. It was lost to South African forces during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa.jpg" alt="German South West Africa" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Imperial Colonial Office of the German Empire Vector: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichskolonialflagge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its direct colonial legacy was modest, comprising German East Africa, <b>German South West Africa</b>, Togo, Cameroon, the leased territory of Kiautschou in China, and Pacific island territories, all stripped by the Treaty of Versailles and largely redistributed as League of Nations mandates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When the First World War began in 1914, Botha’s decision to invade <b>German South West Africa</b> provoked the Maritz Rebellion, a rising of Afrikaner commandos under leaders such as Christiaan de Wet and Manie Maritz, which government forces suppressed by early 1915.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>German South West Africa</b>, after the governor endorsed the shooting of San, over 400 patrols went out in 1911–1913, and the San between Otavi and Gobabis fell from 8,000–12,000 in 1913 to 3,600 in 1923.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>German South West Africa</b> in 1904–1908 most of the Herero and about half of the Nama died in war, in the desert and in camps, which Germany acknowledged as genocide in 2021, and Italy’s reconquest of Libya in 1929–1934 killed tens of thousands in war and concentration…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>German South West Africa</b> Germany committed the Herero and Namaqua genocide, the first genocide of the 20th century.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
